---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
import {
  STANDARDS_PROFILE,
  STANDARDS_PROFILE_VERSION,
  STANDARDS_GOVERNANCE_REVIEWED,
  standardAnchor,
  validateStandardsProfile,
} from '@/lib/spdtf-standards-profile.mjs';
validateStandardsProfile();
const implemented = STANDARDS_PROFILE.filter(({ implementationStatus }) => implementationStatus.startsWith('used'));
---

<Layout title="Standards profile" description="The normative modelling methods and vocabulary profiles selected for SPDTF, their boundaries, and what the Property Pack candidate actually implements.">
  <h1>Standards, vocabularies and modelling frameworks</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This development profile separates three independent facts: the maturity of an
    external specification, the status of an OPDA decision, and whether a candidate
    actually emits or tests the technology. A selected method is binding even when its
    implementation is incomplete; a listed reference is not automatically selected.
  </p>
  <p>
    Profile <code>{STANDARDS_PROFILE_VERSION}</code>. Normative decision reconciliation:
    <time datetime={STANDARDS_GOVERNANCE_REVIEWED}>{STANDARDS_GOVERNANCE_REVIEWED}</time>.
    External-publication checks remain dated separately below; this reconciliation does
    not refresh those checks or alter Property Pack 0.1 package pins.
  </p>

  <h2 id="normative-method">What the selected method requires</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision basis</a> adopts the relevant
    source-method ODRs for concern categories 1, 2, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11. The following
    choices are therefore normative, not a list of possible future approaches. Their
    scope does not extend to excluded concerns, every term in a vocabulary, or wholesale
    ontology imports. The source-method ODR numbers below identify that decision lineage,
    not a claim that its software or business model has been copied into SPDTF.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Selected contracts, separate from implementation progress</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Concern</th><th scope="col">Required approach</th><th scope="col">Boundary and decision basis</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Identity and placement</th><td>Use UFO-informed distinctions between identity-bearing kinds, roles and phases, with OntoClean quality discipline.</td><td>Analytical selection, not imported foundational superclasses. Source-method ODR-0025/0026, ODR-0112/0115/0118.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property applicability</th><td>Document each subject and value side using a safe, universally true RDFS declaration, Schema.org <code>domainIncludes</code>/<code>rangeIncludes</code>, or a governed waiver.</td><td>Do not mix mechanisms on one side. Repeated RDFS domains or ranges entail all declared types; they do not express alternatives. Source-method ODR-0014.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Inference and checking</th><td>Use the selected RDFS/OWL Safe Group, SHACL constraints, and separately declared <code>TripleRule</code>/<code>SPARQLRule</code> materialisation.</td><td>Preserve standard semantics and distinguish asserted from derived information. No unrestricted OWL or blanket SHACL-AF adoption. Source-method ODR-0030/0036.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Reviewed mappings</th><td>Retain the SKOS mapping statement and carry required SSSOM 1.0 provenance, with SEMAPV matching-process values, on a named RDF 1.2 reifier.</td><td>Applies to internal cross-context and external vocabulary mappings. Source-method ODR-0071h, ODR-0087/0096 and ODR-0098 R11.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Provenance and time</th><td>Reuse canonical PROV-O and OWL-Time terms through bounded, locally checked profiles.</td><td>No <code>owl:imports</code>. Separate attribution from truth, and valid time from recorded time. Source-method ODR-0071i/0071j.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Sensitivity and policy</th><td>Use local classification annotations and reviewed DPV concept mappings. Conditionally use ODRL through the DPV Profile of ODRL for the selected policy cases.</td><td>ODRL Common Vocabulary only, with human-curated policies; classification does not enforce access. Source-method ODR-0071k and ODR-0089.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/linked-data-languages">Linked-data languages by concern</a>
    explains why these parts are composed. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/identity-roles-and-phases">identity, roles and phases guide</a>
    explains the analytical method. Their normative status does not imply that every
    generator, shape, mapping record or inference rule has already been implemented.
  </p>

  <h2 id="implemented">What is implemented now</h2>
  <p>
    Property Pack 0.1 is the only current SPDTF implementation example. It uses a
    deliberately small surface: RDF 1.2 Basic-compatible Turtle, RDFS and bounded OWL
    vocabulary, XML Schema datatypes, SKOS, a bounded SHACL 1.2 Core surface and four
    ASK/basic-graph-pattern queries designed for portability but currently tested only in
    ARQ 6.1.0. It also uses limited Dublin Core Terms. The complete shapes graph is exercised
    with one conforming and one non-conforming fixture; that is not per-feature or
    cross-processor conformance. The candidate remains machine-proposed.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Technologies materially used by Property Pack 0.1</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Technology</th><th scope="col">Implementation fact</th><th scope="col">Important non-claim</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {implemented.map((record) => <tr>
        <th scope="row"><a href={record.source}>{record.name}</a></th>
        <td>{record.implementationEvidence}</td>
        <td>{record.conformance}</td>
      </tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3 id="property-pack-feature-boundary">Exact Property Pack 0.1 feature boundary</h3>
  <p>
    This inventory describes emitted terms and executed queries. Parser or validator
    success does not prove entailment, full-profile conformance or semantic correctness.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Terms and features present in the current candidate</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Technology</th><th scope="col">Included surface</th><th scope="col">Explicit boundary</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">RDF</th><td><code>type</code>, written as <code>a</code> in Turtle</td><td>No RDF feature claim beyond the declared Basic-compatible surface.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">RDFS</th><td><code>label</code>, <code>subClassOf</code>, <code>domain</code>, <code>range</code></td><td>No entailment receipt.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">OWL</th><td><code>Ontology</code>, <code>Class</code>, <code>ObjectProperty</code>, <code>DatatypeProperty</code>, <code>AnnotationProperty</code>, <code>versionInfo</code></td><td>No OWL profile or reasoner receipt; <code>sameAs</code> occurs only in a negative query.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">XSD</th><td><code>string</code>, <code>integer</code>, <code>anyURI</code>, <code>boolean</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>dateTime</code>, <code>decimal</code></td><td>No other datatype is declared.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SKOS</th><td><code>ConceptScheme</code>, <code>Concept</code>, <code>prefLabel</code>, <code>definition</code>, <code>inScheme</code>, <code>notation</code></td><td>No hierarchy, mapping, integrity or SKOS-XL claim.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SHACL</th><td><code>NodeShape</code>, <code>targetClass</code>, <code>property</code>, <code>path</code>, <code>datatype</code>, <code>class</code>, <code>minCount</code>, <code>maxCount</code>, <code>pattern</code></td><td>No closed shapes, value lists, Union, AF or SPARQL constraints.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SPARQL</th><td>Four <code>ASK</code> queries using basic graph patterns.</td><td>No other query form or feature.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Dublin Core Terms</th><td><code>description</code>, <code>source</code>, <code>PhysicalResource</code></td><td>PROV-O has a prefix only; no PROV term is emitted.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    PROV-O has a declared prefix but no term use; a prefix is not implementation evidence.
    Its methodological selection is established by the decisions above. There is no
    current Property Pack use of DCAT, DQV, OWL-Time, DPV, ODRL, SSSOM, FIBO,
    GeoSPARQL, Schema.org, Verifiable Credentials, DID, ORG, gUFO or DASH. Cross-context
    mappings remain empty while they await working-group evidence. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">Category 8 mapping guide</a>
    explains the required SKOS and SSSOM contract. An empty candidate mapping set does not
    defer that contract or exempt future internal mappings from it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stack">How the implemented stack fits together</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="The current candidate uses several RDF-based vocabularies in parallel. RDFS/OWL meaning, SKOS concepts and Dublin Core metadata meet in one candidate package; SHACL checks and SPARQL questions inspect that package independently. Projections remain downstream.">
flowchart TB
  accTitle: Property Pack zero point one implemented standards stack
  accDescr: RDF Basic and Turtle provide the substrate for parallel RDFS and OWL meaning, SKOS concepts and Dublin Core metadata; the assembled package is inspected independently by SHACL and SPARQL before downstream projections.
  RDF["RDF 1.2 Basic and Turtle"]:::infra
  OWL["RDFS and bounded OWL 2"]:::service
  SKOS["SKOS concept schemes"]:::data
  DCT["Dublin Core Terms metadata"]:::external
  Package["Versioned candidate package"]:::user
  SHACL["SHACL 1.2 Core checks"]:::security
  SPARQL["ASK / BGP questions in ARQ"]:::process
  Receipt["Technical validation receipt"]:::success
  Projection["Generated technical views"]:::user
  RDF --> OWL
  RDF --> SKOS
  RDF --> DCT
  OWL --> Package
  SKOS --> Package
  DCT --> Package
  Package --> SHACL
  Package --> SPARQL
  SHACL --> Receipt
  SPARQL --> Receipt
  Package --> Projection
  </Diagram>

  <h2 id="maturity">Specification maturity is not OPDA adoption</h2>
  <p>
    As checked on 20 August 2026, RDF 1.2 Concepts is a W3C Candidate Recommendation
    Snapshot. RDF 1.2 Turtle, RDFS 1.2, SHACL 1.2 Core and SPARQL 1.2 Query are Working
    Drafts. OWL 2 and SKOS are W3C Recommendations. Exact candidate snapshots remain
    pinned for reproducibility until a deliberate update reruns feature probes.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Three status axes that must be read together</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Axis</th><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Specification maturity</th><td>What does the owning standards body call this exact snapshot?</td><td>Recommendation, Candidate Recommendation, Working Draft, Note, community report.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">OPDA governance status</th><td>What has SPDTF decided about it?</td><td>Normative selected method, accepted bounded target, conditionally adopted, candidate, deferred, blocked or historical.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Implementation status</th><td>What terms or features does this candidate actually use and test?</td><td>Used and tested, used without a separate conformance receipt, prefix only, or not used.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="mechanisms">Reuse, reference, map and mint</h2>
  <p>
    Only four relationship mechanisms are allowed in the profile. The mechanism describes
    the intended relationship; the implementation field says whether it is realised today.
    Reusing a canonical term does not require importing its entire ontology.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <article class="card"><h3><code>reuse</code></h3><p>Use exact externally governed vocabulary terms, datatypes or classes in the candidate.</p><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> version, term list, licence and governance fit, and feature tests.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3><code>reference</code></h3><p>Use a data-model, syntax, query, specification or method as an implementation or design contract without adopting its vocabulary as SPDTF meaning.</p><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> question addressed, edition and explicit non-conformance boundary.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3><code>map</code></h3><p>Keep local meaning and record a reviewed relation to another term.</p><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> source identities and versions, relation strength, matching-process justification, mapping date and release. Confidence is optional. <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">Read the mapping contract</a>.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h3><code>mint</code></h3><p>Create a locally governed identifier when a reviewed meaning cannot be represented by suitable existing terms.</p><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> a defined meaning, semantic owner, source evidence and approved namespace policy. Machine-proposed candidate IRIs are not governed terms.</p></article>
  </div>

  <h2 id="upper-ontology">Upper ontologies and design methods</h2>
  <p>
    UFO-informed analysis and OntoClean are selected, binding modelling disciplines:
    distinguish stable identity from contingent roles and phases, then check whether
    the proposed hierarchy respects those distinctions. Their selection does not import
    foundational classes, attach domain classes beneath an external upper ontology, or
    establish a full description-logic runtime. The current candidate has no separate
    implementation receipt for these analytical checks.
  </p>
  <p>
    gUFO is a separate OWL implementation and is not imported or used by Property Pack 0.1.
    Selecting the UFO analytical method does not automatically select gUFO terms. DOLCE
    remains historical comparison evidence, not an adopted dependency. See the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-rules#upper">upper-ontology decision boundary</a>
    and <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">normative decision basis</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="register">Detailed standards register</h2>
  <p>
    Every record names a profile boundary, latest checked external maturity, actual implementation,
    OPDA governance status, mechanism, owner, evidence and re-open trigger. The source
    link is the primary specification or method source, not a search result.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid standards-profile-grid">
    {STANDARDS_PROFILE.map((record) => <article class="card">
      <h3 id={standardAnchor(record)}><a href={record.source}>{record.name}</a></h3>
      <dl>
        <dt>Latest checked publication</dt><dd>{record.specificationMaturity} · {record.exactSnapshot}</dd>
        <dt>Selected profile boundary</dt><dd><a href={record.profileSource}>{record.versionBoundary}</a></dd>
        <dt>Property Pack 0.1 pin</dt><dd>{record.candidateSnapshot}</dd>
        <dt>Implementation</dt><dd>{record.implementationStatus}. {record.implementationEvidence}</dd>
        <dt>Governance</dt><dd>{record.governanceStatus}</dd>
        <dt>Purpose</dt><dd>{record.purpose}</dd>
        <dt>Feature boundary</dt><dd>{record.conformance}</dd>
        <dt>Mechanism</dt><dd><code>{record.mechanism}</code></dd>
        <dt>Owner</dt><dd>{record.owner}</dd>
        <dt>Decision evidence</dt><dd>{record.evidence}</dd>
        <dt>Re-open trigger</dt><dd>{record.reopenTrigger}</dd>
        <dt>External publication last checked</dt><dd>{record.lastChecked}</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>)}
  </div>

  <h2 id="projections">Projections are not profile mechanisms</h2>
  <p>
    JSON Schema, JSON-LD, forms, APIs, website pages, PDF and Markdown are possible future
    projections. Property Pack 0.1 currently emits RDF/OWL and SKOS graphs, SHACL shapes,
    JSON registers/model views, HTML documentation, ASK queries and a validation report;
    it does not emit all of those possible projections. Any future projection's package
    version and validation belong in the
    <a href="/development/outputs">outputs register</a>; “projection” is not a fifth standards
    relationship mechanism.
  </p>
</Layout>
